A||||4||Philphine||painting in pieces over time||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||08-18-2001||09:56 AM||||<BR>i know disasembly painting has been covered, but i'm wondering if i need to consider the time gap bettween getting parts painted. i'm sure i won't get the whole car taken apart but things like my wing, indy scoop, and facias (maybe a thing or two else) i think i want to get painted off the car.<P> if i'm going with black (for my '87 coupe. still dreaming for my chopper), will it matter how long the time period is bettween starting and finishing as far as color matching is concerned? what about other colors or even a mystic type job? do i just need to make sure the paint shop i go to has a way of keeping up with the color i want each time i go in? how hard would that generaly be for them?<P>i'm hoping to do it this way to both get a better paint job and more easily afford it. thanks for any help.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>a man of constant sorrow||||reg||1
Z||000001||hyhy4u||08-18-2001||10:09 AM||||Well it all depends on how long is the time interval your looking at...paint wont fade overnight..it will take a while to do it.  It also plays in how much time your car spends out time in the "elements".  Like my car is garage kept and hardly ever in the rain or sun, and yes im also thinking of taking this path since some of my panels look bad but others look good. But you also gotta consider over that time..your gonna have a lot of down time..can you afford that??, a lot more down time of your car then if you would just do it at once..all things to keep in mind  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||SQL||08-18-2001||10:50 AM||||If you have your paint mixed for you make sure you have enough to do the entire car with some left over for spot repairs.<P><P>------------------<BR>stuck at work||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-18-2001||01:14 PM||||things like wing and scoop should be painted off car. Only thing i dont like to paint disassemled is the main body parts. Depending on color, anytime you paint parts theres a very good chance it wont match painting over an extended period. In fact if you paint 2 parts a day apart out of the same can its possible to get a mismatch. Spraying has so many variables that change colors to some extent. Humidity, temp, thinness of paint, air pressure all have some effect. Id always wait and try to paint as much as possible at one time.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-18-2001||01:24 PM||||I have to agree with allot of the above....<P>you aren't going to have much if any of a fading problem.<P>The hard part will be getting consistent finishes. Solid colors won't be as bad as something like a metal flake.<P>No matter what paint shop you use, there will be problems getting things even if it's not done all at once. There will always be small variations in how the paint is thinned and stuff like that. Every little thing will affect the final work.<P>Unless you are doing a color change, I'd leave most stuff on the car except for wing/rack and trim. You'll always get better results if the trim stuff is off plus you don't risk loose crap under the trim blowing out into the wet paint.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-18-2001).]||||reg||1
